    text: 'Open Admin → Frameworks to browse the catalogue. Search names and descriptions, narrow by region or Enabled only , and page through the cards. Tick the frameworks your organisation uses, choose the organisation default and select Save organisation selection . Open a card’s Edit action to reach its node editor; Back to catalogue returns to the cards. The framework catalogue: search, regional filters, enabled frameworks and per-framework Edit and Compare actions. Compare opens a before-and-after comparison of two catalogues. Review added, removed and changed nodes, filter entries or show unchanged entries. Matching uses category and reference: a renumbered node appears as a removal and addition. The comparison concerns saved catalogue content and structure; it does not assess evidence or certify equivalent requirements. Framework comparison: additions, removals and changed catalogue entries between the selected versions.  The framework editor: the node being edited is highlighted in the tree while its reference, title, parent and scope are changed on the right.  Administrators maintain frameworks in Admin → Frameworks . Everyday work there is small: pick a framework, adjust a node, save.  Choose Select framework to edit an existing framework, or New framework to create one. Enter a slug, name, version, description and optional upstream URL, then Save framework . Choose New node to add a Clause, Control or Custom node. Give it a reference and title, then a description, an optional parent of the same category, an upstream URL, a sort order and its In scope setting. A control can also link to related clauses. Choose Save node . To edit a node, select it in the searchable tree. The node you are editing is marked in the tree and stays marked after you save.  Create a replacement to change an identity. A slug, a reference and a category identify a framework or node across the whole application, so the editor does not rename them. To change one, create the replacement and move the relationships deliberately.  Going deeper: deleting nodes and licensed text Deleting a node is blocked while it has child nodes, clause links or evidence mappings. Review those relationships first. When history must survive, mark the node out of scope; deleting it removes that history. Use upstream links to point at authorised guidance. Paste licensed standard text only when your organisation has the right to store and share it. ',
