    title: 'Create a mapping',
    book: 'Using Keen',
    url: 'using-mapping.html#5-configure-collection-and-mapping-with-evidence-definitions',
    text: " The editor is in Admin → Sources & evidence mapping . A mapping, also called an evidence definition elsewhere in these guides, combines three things: a source input, the events from it that count as evidence, and the controls or clauses they support. One input can feed several mappings, and one event can support several frameworks.  Three words describe the pieces. An adapter connects to a service. A collection item is one input from it: a query, job, feed, project or page. A mapping says which events from that input support which controls. Reuse collection items when several mappings share an input.  Choose Create mapping to open the three-step editor. The example below follows a mapping for “Successful production deployments recorded by the approved deployment job.”  Step 1: Matching events  Step 1, Matching events: choose the source, copy the match values from a recent event, then narrow which events match.  Under Where does the evidence come from? , choose the source and, where it has several, the connection. Open Limit to a particular query, job, feed or page to restrict the mapping to one collection item, or to set one up. Leave it unset only when every input from that source belongs. Under Start from a real event , pick a Recent event and choose Copy its match values . The conditions take the exact values the event recorded, including distinctions such as “deploy” and “deployment”. Adjust Which events should match? Every condition you fill in has to match, and a blank box allows any value. Use Structured event fields and More filters for narrower conditions.  Prefer system, action, outcome, actor, severity or label values you can see on real events, and keep regex for patterns exact values cannot express.  Step 2: Name and controls  Step 2, Name and controls: name what the evidence demonstrates, then add each control or clause it supports; suggestions come from the name you typed.  Give the Mapping name as a statement of what the matching events demonstrate. Pick a framework and a control or clause, then choose Add . Repeat for each target, including targets in other frameworks. The framework list starts on the one selected in the navigation bar. Optionally choose Suggest relevant controls . Suggestions come from the mapping name and are candidates for you to review.  Only targets in the list are saved: a control still selected in the picker without Add is left out, and Next waits until the list has at least one target.  Step 3: Check and publish  Step 3, Check and publish: the summary of the mapping, then Preview matches shows which recent events match and explains, for each non-match, which condition failed.  Check the Summary . Each line has a Change link back to its step. Choose Preview matches . Green Matches entries show the event and its proposed framework targets; amber Does not match entries explain why an event was excluded. Labels accompany the colours. Test a positive sample and an event that should not qualify. Make sure another collection item, a failed outcome or an unrelated environment cannot match by accident; if one does, go back and adjust the conditions. Under Advanced rule settings , review the confidence, whether the rule is enabled and whether to apply it to previously collected evidence, then choose Save rule . A background job runs separately from the save itself.  Confidence is your own mapping confidence between 0 and 1; compliance is assessed through evidence and review. While a source type is paused in the source catalogue, its existing evidence stays available for review and mapping.  Example. If the inspected event says system “production-api”, action “deployment” and outcome “success”, those three conditions restrict the mapping to successful deployments of that system. A failed deployment deserves its own mapping or an incident investigation; sharing the success mapping because both events came from Jenkins would blur the evidence.  Mappings before events arrive  Tick Predefine this rule before evidence is available under Advanced rule settings when there is no sample to preview. As soon as collection runs, preview the mapping against the first real events and correct any assumption about field values or collection identity.  Collection settings and credentials  A Jenkins job's kind becomes the event action and its label the event system. For Loki, configure the query and its normalised event fields. For BookStack, select a book and then a page, grouped by chapter, using the picker's page-loading buttons when needed; the picker uses the server's configured credentials.  Additional input settings (advanced) takes connector-specific JSON options, and Shared adapter settings holds advanced options common to an adapter. Neither is a place for secrets: connection credentials and adapter enablement are server settings for the hosting operator.  Historical evidence and changing rules  Rules change, and evidence already collected should follow. Apply saved rule to past evidence runs a background job that reconciles the automatic mappings this rule owns: it adds matching controls and removes links to controls you removed from the rule, including links on events that no longer match.  What stays untouched: manual and imported mappings, links still supported by another active rule, and older mappings whose rule ownership is uncertain. When the job finishes, reopen an event to review its controls.  Going deeper: remapping a wider range Admin → Sources & evidence mapping → Evidence mapping → Remap old events supports a wider operation with source and time filters. Its normal mode finds unmapped events; Include already-mapped events permits additive mappings. Begin with a bounded range and inspect the results. ",
