    title: 'Maintain the ISMS',
    book: 'Using Keen',
    url: 'using-isms.html#11-maintain-the-isms',
    text: " Objectives Intended outcomes, linked to controls, owners and effectiveness measures. Effectiveness measures and metrics A measure says how effectiveness is assessed; a metric is one dated observation or score for it. Documents Policies and processes, with folders, types, tags, rich text, version history and comments. Assets The resources you manage, with ownership, organisation relationships and related risks. Organisation chart Roles and reporting relationships. Roles can own risks. Access Matrix Who, or which role, should have access to an asset and at what level. It records intended access; grants and revocations happen in the external system. Application Configuration Relevant application settings, documented. Minutes of meetings Decisions, discussion and attendance, including people without Keen accounts.  Effectiveness measures: the latest value against its target, who owns the measure, the control it informs and the metric history behind it.  Before you add a metric, review the measure's method, target and source. Some metrics arrive through configured webhooks. Where a measure records automatic zero values, read them as part of its method and check source coverage separately.  Save substantive document changes as a new content version, so the history shows which wording was in force at any time. If someone else edits at the same time, a reload reconciles their changes.  Going deeper: how ISMS links become evidence Linking an ISMS record to framework controls or clauses records an automatic Diary entry for the record. The entry lists its current control and clause references and counts towards the linked controls' evidence. Risks and interested parties linked to controls work the same way, so each record linked to a control counts towards the control coverage on Home and the Controls page. Revising the links updates the entry and credits each added mapping to the person who saved the record.  People and vendors People register filtered to fictional demo people, with roles, optional Keen accounts and assurance-record counts. Personnel assurance: training and acknowledgements with status, source, completion, expiry and evidence. Vendors register: supplier contacts, descriptions and supplied-asset counts. A vendor’s own page separates its details, supplied assets and related items. Open a person for Details , Workstation posture , Personnel assurance , Assets and Related items . Workstation posture summarises reports from linked workstation agents; an empty page means no such devices are linked. A check reads Not established when current reports do not confirm the setting. For example, Windows switches the screen saver on by default without selecting one, so that alone does not establish a screen lock. Not collected means the device reported that it could not read the setting on its last run. Where your organisation sets its own targets, such as how soon a screen must lock, each result is judged against them; each observation shows the measured value and the target beside its result, and links to the signed evidence. Open a vendor for its Details , Assets and Related items .  A check that needs attention offers Create a risk to anyone who manages risks. The form below the device's checks starts with a title and a threat summary drawn from the evidence; choose a treatment, an owner and a review date, then save. Keen adds the risk to the workstation's asset, creating that asset under End-user devices › Workstations and linking it to the person the first time, and links the risk to the controls the evidence maps to in the selected framework. Open the risk afterwards to rate it or change its treatment.  To accept the risk, choose Accept with an owner and a review date. Until that date, the check reads Risk accepted and shows the risk beside it; the device's evidence and its count of met checks stay unchanged. After the review date, or once the risk is closed or treated another way, the check needs attention again and the risk shows Acceptance expired . People without risk access see that a risk is recorded, without its title.  People & vendors holds personnel and supplier assurance. A person does not need a Keen account: record their optional account, organisation role and assets, then add assurance records such as training, screening or policy acknowledgement. Each record can carry a category, requirement, status, completion and expiry dates, an authoritative source and an evidence link.  A vendor record holds supplier details and the assets it supplies. An asset has one supplying vendor, so assigning it to another vendor moves that relationship; review the effect before saving.  A vendor that supplies a cloud service Keen reads, such as AWS, Microsoft or Google, has a Cloud posture tab. It shows how each account is set up, with checks such as MFA for console users, administrators and unused users, each linked to its evidence, the identities the provider lists and the provider's recent activity. An identity whose email matches a person in the People register links to that person. A check that needs attention, or a user or resource it lists, offers Create a risk ; the risk goes on the account's asset and, accepted with an owner and a review date, shows the check as Risk accepted until that date. Administrators turn providers on and can refresh them (see Cloud vendor posture ). ",
