    text: "Start an audit: enter a title, choose internal or external, and add optional dates. Scope step: selecting a policy also selects its linked clauses and controls; scope can be changed later. Check and create: review details, scope and attendees, and use Change to return to an earlier step.  Open Audits → Start an audit . The audit belongs to the framework selected in the top navigation, so switch that first if you mean another one. Give the audit a title you will recognise in the register, say whether it is an internal or an external audit, and add its begin and end dates if you know them. Tick the controls, clauses, policies and procedures the audit will examine, and the SWOT items if you have ISMS access. Choosing a policy, procedure or clause adds the clauses and controls linked to it. You can skip this step and set the scope later on the audit's page. Add the participants, preferably from the people register or from Keen accounts, with each person's role. Check names and contact details before any invitations go out. Check the summary, use Change to correct a step, and create the audit. Then choose where to start: look for evidence by event, by control or in the ISMS, or start writing the report notes. Evidence you sample goes to this audit unless you pick another. From an event or other supported record, use its sampling action to add it to the audit. Explain the selection and your assessment in the notes. Compare each requirement with the sampled facts and add findings with the requirement, observation and needed action. Maintain the report notes and executive summary on the audit's page, during the audit and at its end. Attach an external report with Upload report or add supporting URLs. Use Export audit ZIP and read the export. When you are satisfied that scope, participants, samples and findings hang together, complete the audit.  An audit's page: details and status, the final report, and counts of evidence, scope, people and findings.  Keep the three ideas apart. A linked control is a scope statement, a sample is the material you examined, and a finding is your assessment. Choose samples that cover the important requirements and the relevant period. ",
