    title: 'Select evidence and finish an audit',
    book: 'Using Keen',
    url: 'using-audits.html#audit-sampling-detail',
    text: " Use Open while preparing an audit and In progress while conducting it. Scope says what you plan to cover; samples record what you examined and what you found.  Sampled evidence: each sample keeps its link, the controls it supports and audit-specific notes.  Choose the material to sample  On an evidence event or another supported record, choose Sample into audit , pick the audit and add a title and notes: why this sample, which requirement, what you observed. Sampling the same record again updates its existing sample. Removing a sample only removes its association with the audit; the event or record itself is untouched.  Audit scope: SWOT items, ISMS documents, clauses and controls are ticked into scope and saved together.  You can sample evidence events, risks, PESTLE(E) items, interested parties, objectives, documents, BookStack policy snapshots, organisation-chart nodes, assets, access-matrix rows, application-configuration rows, meeting minutes, effectiveness measures, individual metric entries and stories. An audit also accepts supporting URL evidence. For context and response reviews, include SWOT items in scope and sample them .  Going deeper: samples are references Most sampled ISMS records are references to a record that can change after sampling. When you need a point-in-time record, keep the applicable native document version or a captured artefact. A BookStack policy snapshot carries its own captured revision and checksum and is sampled under its target framework.  Record findings and conclusions  A finding is a Major non-conformity , Minor non-conformity , Opportunity for improvement or Best practice . Give it a title and description, optionally a control, and keep its status as Open , Accepted or Closed . Write the requirement, the sampled observation and the conclusion so another reviewer can follow the reasoning.  Maintain the report notes and executive summary, add supporting links and upload the final report (up to 100 MiB). Review participants, scope, samples and findings before completion; Completed and Archived audits restrict edits.  Going deeper: what the ZIP export contains Export audit ZIP renders the audit page as a PDF, includes the uploaded final report when present, and downloads the attachments of sampled evidence events. Linked external content and other ISMS records are not captured; include them separately when the export needs them. Inspect the export, including any configured masking and PDF footer, before distributing it.  Schedule the next audit  Scheduled audits holds templates that create ordinary Open audits. A template chooses an exact date, the first weekday of a month or the first named Monday-Friday, and recurs once, weekly, monthly, quarterly or yearly with an interval and optional end. Keep scope and attendees on the template; selected documents can contribute their linked clauses and controls when an occurrence is created.  Going deeper: timing and dependencies Month-based first-weekday rules cannot be combined with weekly recurrence. Date-only scheduling uses the server's KEEN_TIMEZONE , and the administrator sets how many days ahead occurrences are created. The worker and scheduler must be running for creation to happen, and configured mail delivers notifications and calendar information; verify delivery after saving a template. An occurrence that already exists is a separate audit record, so review it when you change its template. ",
