    text: ' This chapter gets a self-managed Keen running: what the containers do, how to deploy and upgrade, the settings that matter on day one, and the Features switches that decide which parts of Keen your organisation sees.  Keen runs as a small set of services:  Service Role keen-ui Serves the interface and proxies API requests. keen-api The FastAPI application. Inbound webhooks, agent events and interactive catalogue lookups run here. keen-worker Consumes the celery queue for internal jobs: notifications, cache refreshes, mapping backfills. keen-http-worker Consumes ingest-http for built-in HTTP collectors, named connections and custom API ingester runs. keen-beat Schedules the recurring work. PostgreSQL, Valkey Records; sessions, caches, job messaging and notifications. The optional IMAP profile adds its own worker and queue.  Admin ingest runners queue collection and return a task ID. A queued confirmation means the job was accepted, so check the worker results and logs to see it finish.  Artefacts live in the configured local storage or S3-compatible storage, and the API and worker must see the same artefacts. PostgreSQL holds the references, mappings and configuration that give those objects meaning, so a complete backup includes both.  Expose only the UI listener. The external HTTPS proxy forwards to the UI listener (127.0.0.1:8081 in the supplied Compose file). PostgreSQL, Valkey and the API are never exposed directly, and the proxy scrubs client-supplied identity headers.  Going deeper: resources, capabilities and upgrades The ordinary and HTTP workers each default to one child process; the HTTP worker is limited to 768 MiB, one CPU and 128 PIDs. Monitor host capacity and queue age when tuning concurrency. Python containers drop all Linux capabilities and prevent privilege escalation; normal network connections need none. Keep the artifact and scheduler volumes writable by the image user, UID 10001. During an upgrade, stop scheduling and manual submissions and let collection jobs on the old default queue finish. Routing changes apply to new jobs only; do not purge existing jobs. Deploy matching API, worker and scheduler images and start keen-http-worker with them. Hosted demo provisioning also includes this worker. The standard Compose configuration includes pgdata, beatdata and artifacts volumes. ',
