    text: ' Setting Purpose KEEN_DATABASE_URL , KEEN_REDIS_URL PostgreSQL and Valkey connections. KEEN_PUBLIC_BASE_URL Required. The externally reachable origin, without a path, used for links in e-mails and messages and for browser origin checks. Keen does not start without it. KEEN_TIMEZONE , KEEN_UI_DATE_FORMAT Installation defaults; users can set their own presentation preferences. KEEN_LOCAL_AUTH_ENABLED Whether local sign-in is offered. KEEN_COOKIE_SECURE , KEEN_COOKIE_SAMESITE , KEEN_CSRF_COOKIE_SAMESITE , KEEN_COOKIE_DOMAIN Cookie policy. Keep Secure on in production, pick SameSite values that suit your sign-in flow and test the full cross-site OIDC flow, and leave the domain empty for host-only cookies unless a reviewed deployment needs otherwise. KEEN_SESSION_TTL_SECONDS and the session-cookie name Session lifetime. KEEN_SECURITY_TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS The proxies in front of the API, including the supplied UI proxy. Without it every client shares the proxy address and per-address login limits apply to the whole installation.  Native OIDC, Google and GitHub  Native OIDC uses KEEN_OIDC_ENABLED , the issuer, authorisation and token endpoints, the JWKS URI, client ID, client secret, scopes and an exact redirect URI. The redirect URI sits on KEEN_PUBLIC_BASE_URL and ends in /api/v1/auth/sso/oidc/callback , /api/v1/auth/sso/google/callback or /api/v1/auth/sso/github/callback for the provider concerned. Register the same address with the provider; Keen refuses provider sign-in until each enabled provider has its redirect URI set.  Google and GitHub have their own enablement, client and claim settings. Provision authorised local identities according to the selected mode, and keep automatic provisioning restricted to the identities you intend to admit.  Going deeper: token endpoint method, trusted proxies and hosted mode KEEN_OIDC_TOKEN_ENDPOINT_AUTH_METHOD selects how Keen presents its client secret to the token endpoint: client_secret_basic (the default) or client_secret_post . Configure the matching logout URLs where supported, and keep TLS verification and the asymmetric algorithm allowlist. Trusted remote-user mode requires a direct peer in KEEN_SECURITY_TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS and a proxy that strips incoming identity headers and supplies exactly one verified identity. Keep Uvicorn proxy-header rewriting disabled, as in the supplied startup script, and never trust a header from an internet client. Hosted mode disables local and trusted-header authentication and requires its configured owner subject and OIDC issuer. Login rate limiting uses the configured window and per-IP and per-username limits, which makes Valkey availability security-critical as well as operationally important. ',
