    title: 'Delivery and troubleshooting',
    book: 'Administering Keen',
    url: 'administrator-identity.html#security-notification-queue',
    text: " A change and its notification intent commit together in PostgreSQL. Every minute the worker processes up to ten queued messages and retries SMTP failures up to eight times with increasing delays capped at one hour, so delivery never runs inside a login or account-change request. SMTP acceptance means the relay took the message; mailbox delivery is the mail service's job.  Going deeper: inspecting the outbox The worker logs notification IDs, attempt counts and status, without SMTP exception text or message contents. Operators can inspect the security_notifications table for status , attempts , next_attempt_at and last_error , and requeue failed rows after fixing SMTP. Sent and failed rows are removed after thirty days while SMTP is configured. A crash after SMTP acceptance and before recording success can produce a duplicate notification. Passwords, TOTP secrets and recovery-code values never appear in these messages. ",
