    title: 'SSO email addresses and account linking',
    book: 'Administering Keen',
    url: 'administrator-sso-ldap.html#sso-email-linking',
    text: " An SSO identity is matched to a Keen account by its issuer and subject. This chapter explains how a second identity can be linked through a verified e-mail address, and how directory (LDAP) sign-in works alongside local accounts.  To link another identity automatically, its provider must report a verified e-mail address that exactly matches an address verified in Keen; usernames never authorise linking. Comparison ignores case and surrounding whitespace. Provider domain restrictions and the provider's auto-link setting also apply.  Configure SMTP and an HTTPS KEEN_PUBLIC_BASE_URL . Users open Account → Security → SSO email addresses , enter a primary or additional address they own, and send a verification link. Adding or removing an address needs a sign-in within the last five minutes. They open the link while signed into the requesting account and confirm it within thirty minutes. Each link works once; a new request replaces it.  Going deeper: limits and edge cases Each address belongs to one account, each account may verify up to ten addresses, and verification requests are limited to five per hour. Existing contact addresses must also be verified before they can authorise a new SSO link. Password and MFA changes invalidate pending links, and Keen stores only a hash of the verification token. Verified addresses and existing identity links keep working during an SMTP outage; removing an address stops future automatic linking through it without removing existing associations. A user who has never been linked needs an authenticated way in to verify an address: arrange local onboarding before requiring SSO exclusively, or provision a trusted issuer and subject association through your provisioning process. SSO sign-in transactions are bound to the initiating browser and provider, expire after ten minutes and are consumed once, so a user who opened another attempt, or began one before an upgrade, restarts sign-in. KEEN_SECURITY_TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS governs forwarding-header interpretation for security e-mails, audit records and IP-based rate limits. Trust only your actual proxy hops; with none configured, Keen records and limits the socket peer address. ",
