    url: 'administrator-sso-ldap.html#ldap-directory-auth',
    text: " Directory sign-in lets people use their existing directory password. Keen still owns the account's permissions and its second factor.  In .env , set KEEN_LDAP_ENABLED=true , KEEN_LDAP_URL , KEEN_LDAP_BIND_DN , KEEN_LDAP_BIND_PASSWORD and KEEN_LDAP_BASE_DN . Use a read-only service account for searching. A base such as ou=People,dc=example,dc=org limits logins to that OU and its descendants. Use ldap://host:389 for mandatory StartTLS or ldaps://host:636 for LDAPS; both verify the certificate and hostname. For a private CA, mount its PEM certificate into the API container and set KEEN_LDAP_CA_CERT_PATH to that container path, such as /app/config/ldap-ca.pem . Recreate the API container and test with a directory user while a local administrator stays available.  Setting Default Notes KEEN_LDAP_USERNAME_ATTRIBUTE uid Active Directory commonly uses sAMAccountName . KEEN_LDAP_EMAIL_ATTRIBUTE mail KEEN_LDAP_ID_ATTRIBUTE entryUUID A stable identity; Active Directory commonly uses objectGUID . KEEN_LDAP_USER_FILTER A directory-specific restriction. Exactly one entry must match. KEEN_LDAP_AUTO_PROVISION false allows only previously provisioned LDAP identities.  A successful LDAP login creates an ordinary Keen account with a separate directory identity, and administrators assign its permissions. Matching e-mail addresses or usernames do not link local and LDAP accounts, and changing the LDAP server URL means planning an identity migration.  Going deeper: MFA, passwords and disablement LDAP logins follow Keen's MFA policy; users enter their directory password to manage authenticators, security keys and recovery codes in Account → Security. Directory passwords are changed in the directory. Disabling the Keen user blocks application access immediately; directory disablement and OU restrictions take effect at the next password authentication, and existing sessions keep Keen's configured lifetime. An empty KEEN_LDAP_CA_CERT_PATH uses system trust. ",
