    title: 'Install and enrol on a Linux server',
    book: 'Administering Keen',
    url: 'administrator-agents.html#agent-build-and-install-on-a-linux-server',
    text: " Install the signed package from the repository described at keen.cloud/agent : apt install keen-agent on Debian and Ubuntu, or dnf install keen-agent on Fedora and RHEL-compatible systems. The package creates the keen-agent service account, the configuration directory and the hardened systemd unit. Keep the configuration and the state directory during an upgrade.  Edit /etc/keen-agent/config.yaml : set endpoint , enable only sources that exist on the server and choose an evidence_mode . Save the enrolment code as a single line, readable by the service only: sudo install -o root -g keen-agent -m 0640 /dev/null /etc/keen-agent/enrollment-code\nsudoedit /etc/keen-agent/enrollment-code Grant the service account read access to the selected logs. For journald, where the group exists: sudo usermod -aG systemd-journal keen-agent Check, enrol, start and watch: sudo -u keen-agent keen-agent -command check\nsudo -u keen-agent keen-agent -command enroll\nsudo rm /etc/keen-agent/enrollment-code\nsudo systemctl enable --now keen-agent\nsudo journalctl -u keen-agent -f\nsudo -u keen-agent keen-agent -command status  check validates configuration, file permissions and CA loading. enroll generates the agent's Ed25519 key pair in /var/lib/keen-agent/identity.json (mode 0600), checks that the server holds the Keen identity key named at the end of the code, sends Keen the public key with proof that the agent holds the private key, encrypted to Keen, and saves the binding Keen returns. The service also enrols on its first start if a code file is configured. status reads the latest local report while the service runs. The service reports health to Keen about once a minute.  Keep codes and identities out of arguments, YAML and shell history. The private key never leaves identity.json ; back up the state directory with the same protection as other host secrets, or re-enrol a replacement machine with a new code. Avoid granting write access, CAP_AUDIT_CONTROL or CAP_DAC_READ_SEARCH ; the shipped service runs without capabilities.  Going deeper: manual builds, file permissions and the systemd unit Keen Agent is maintained in its own repository, whose release guide describes the packages for each platform and how to verify their signatures. A manual build installs the same files: build with the Go version in go.mod , create a keen-agent system account with home /var/lib/keen-agent , install the binary to /usr/bin/keen-agent , examples/config.yaml to /etc/keen-agent/config.yaml (owned by root:keen-agent , mode 0640) and packaging/keen-agent.service as the systemd unit. For files, use a dedicated log-reader group or narrowly scoped ACLs. Auditd commonly restricts both /var/log/audit traversal and the log file itself: configure its log_group and directory permissions according to local policy, and ensure logrotate and auditd rotation create replacement files with the same read access. The service uses /var/lib/keen-agent with mode 0700. The systemd unit limits privileges, memory, process count and writable paths; keep state_dir at its default unless you also update the unit's StateDirectory and ReadWritePaths , and test the unit against your distribution's security policy (including SELinux/AppArmor). ",
