    title: 'Signed evidence and its checks',
    book: 'Administering Keen',
    url: 'administrator-agents.html#agent-signed-evidence',
    text: " Every envelope follows Keen's profile of the draft UEU specification, 0.1-keen . It names its event type and the catalogue that defines it, the agent and asset that produced it, the outcome, typed facts or attributes, a pseudonymous subject where the type allows one, the privacy transformations applied at source and its place in the agent's hash chain. Its evidence hash is the SHA-256 of its RFC 8785 canonical JSON.  The agent signs a manifest listing every envelope's evidence ID and hash in each batch, encrypts the request to Keen, and signs it (RFC 9421 HTTP Message Signatures over the method, operation, encryption key, encrypted body digest and content type). Keen checks, in order:  the request signature, its time (within five minutes), its single use and that it was made for this endpoint, then opens the encrypted body; that each envelope is exactly its canonical form, follows the profile and, for a catalogue Keen holds, uses only that type's declared fields with their declared types and none of its prohibited fields; the batch manifest signature and that the manifest lists exactly the batch's envelopes; each envelope's place in the agent's chain.  A failure in steps 1 to 3 rejects the whole request, so nothing from it is stored. The event page shows the outcome of each check under Evidence provenance :  Result Meaning Signature verified The batch was signed with a key Keen holds for its producer. Unsigned The producer sent no batch signature. The content is as received. Signed by an unknown key The batch is signed with a key Keen does not hold, so the signature could not be checked. Continuous, stream started, gap, restarted, chain broken, out of order How the envelope follows the previous one Keen received from the agent. A gap means envelopes in between did not arrive; the agent sends an evidence.records.discarded envelope when it discards queued evidence, which accounts for the gap. Catalogue checked, or catalogue not held Whether Keen validated the typed fields against the named catalogue, or only the envelope structure. Stored as signed, or changes by Keen Whether Keen's redaction changed anything before storage, with each changed path and the reason.  Booleans, integers and timestamps cannot hold a secret, so Keen keeps typed measurements such as password_required: true whatever their names, and redacts text with its usual rules. When Keen changes an envelope, the stored copy no longer hashes to the evidence hash; its custody record keeps the original hash and lists the change. With no changes, the stored artifact is the exact signed bytes.  Each agent event's page shows the results of these checks: the batch signature, the place in the agent's chain, the catalogue validation and any changes Keen made before storage, with the evidence ID, hash, sequence and signing key. Reviewers read them there; Keen does not package evidence for offline verification.  Going deeper: catalogues An event catalogue is the implementor's vocabulary, much as OpenID Connect leaves claim names to implementations. Keen ships its own catalogue, keen@2026.10 , and accepts envelopes naming another catalogue with structural checks only. Operators can install further catalogues as JSON files in KEEN_UEU_CATALOGUE_DIR (default /app/config/ueu/catalogues ); Keen loads them at start-up and refuses duplicates and catalogues that declare personal data or authentication secrets as sendable fields. Any signed-in account can list and download the catalogues Keen holds ( published catalogues ). ",
