    text: ' app/services/features.py defines stable feature keys, defaults, parent dependencies and entity mappings. GET /v1/admin/features returns configured and effective settings; PUT /v1/admin/features accepts an expected version and a changes object of known boolean settings. Both require administrator authority, and normal CSRF, authentication and request limits apply.  DomainRoute applies the shared feature dependency to matched route owners and entity parameters while existing permission checks remain authoritative. /v1/me supplies the effective feature map during bootstrap, the shared frontend feature helper filters navigation and marked elements, and page loaders skip disabled subsections; the frontend checks are presentation only. Every new route or cross-feature operation needs an availability decision and regression coverage alongside its permission inventory entry.  Going deeper: how the setting is stored and cached The document and its revisions use the existing managed configuration tables. Saving locks the current row and checks the revision, a competing initial insert is rejected by the primary key, and successful writes commit the document and revision together and invalidate the local compiled cache. Each new guarded request reads the authoritative database revision, and a worker reloads the bounded immutable document only when its revision changes. The cache is scoped to its database bind and contains no account identity or permission decisions; database and configuration errors fail closed. This avoids a stale time-to-live window and adds one small indexed revision lookup per guarded request, however many features it checks. Public metric ingestion checks availability after its normal rate-limit and credential checks, domain operations separately guard sampling, new scope associations and scheduled work, and existing historical audit references are retained. ',
